      <!-- ==== FORMULA ==== -->
      <section class="bg-slate-50 border-y border-slate-200">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
            The formulas, explained in plain English
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Rainwater is a volume caught over a period, then a storage question. Grey water is a flow you
            already have, and the only real constraint on it is time.
          </p>

          <div
            class="mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 p-6 sm:p-8 text-slate-100 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"
          >
            <div class="text-cyan-300"># What the catchment yields</div>
            <div>
              gal&nbsp;=&nbsp;area (ft²) × rain (in) ×
              <span class="text-amber-300">{fmt(GAL_PER_SQFT_PER_INCH, 5)}</span> × Cr × Ce
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 text-slate-400"># Where that constant comes from — not a table</div>
            <div>1 in over 1 ft²&nbsp;=&nbsp;1/12 ft³</div>
            <div>
              × <span class="text-amber-300">{GALLONS_PER_CUBIC_FOOT}</span> gal/ft³&nbsp;=&nbsp;
              <span class="text-amber-300">{fmt(GAL_PER_SQFT_PER_INCH, 5)}</span> gal
            </div>
            <div class="text-slate-400">
              ÷ 60 gives {fmt(1 / ROOF_RUNOFF_DIVISOR, 7)} gpm — the storm calculator's {fmt(ROOF_RUNOFF_DIVISOR, 2)} divisor
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 text-cyan-300"># The cistern — the SMALLER of two limits</div>
            <div>demand-limited&nbsp;=&nbsp;daily demand × dry-spell days</div>
            <div>yield-limited&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=&nbsp;what the roof delivers that month</div>
            <div class="text-slate-400">storing past your own catchment buys an empty shell</div>
            <div class="mt-4 text-cyan-300"># Grey water — a flow, not a store</div>
            <div>gpd&nbsp;=&nbsp;Σ (occupants × per-person yield) + loads/wk × gal ÷ 7</div>
            <div>
              max hold&nbsp;=&nbsp;<span class="text-amber-300">{GREYWATER_MAX_STORAGE_HOURS} hours</span>
              &nbsp;→&nbsp;tank&nbsp;=&nbsp;one day
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 text-red-300"># What this does NOT do</div>
            <div>no jurisdiction table&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;no treatment or disinfection design</div>
            <div class="text-slate-400">potable reuse is out of scope entirely</div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-8 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Two sources, two questions</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
                A cistern separates supply from demand <em>in time</em>, so it is sized on how long the gap
                is. A grey water tank cannot do that at all — it is a surge vessel that has to empty daily.
                Sizing one with the other's method gives a plausible number that means nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">The dry spell, not the roof</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
                Across every roof material except a green roof, these defaults land on the same
                {" "}{fmt(base.selected ?? 0, 0)} gallon tank. Move the dry spell from
                {" "}{fmt(demandGoverned[0].days, 0)} days to {fmt(demandGoverned[demandGoverned.length - 1].days, 0)}
                {" "}and the requirement moves about {fmt(drySpellSwing, 1)}×. Material is a rounding error
                next to duration.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Cross-connection control is mandatory</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
                Any nonpotable system with a potable top-up is a cross connection. It needs an approved
                assembly, purple pipe and labelled outlets — and remember that
                {" "}<a href="/calculators/plumbing/backflow-prevention" class="font-semibold text-cyan-700 hover:underline">no vacuum breaker stops backpressure</a>,
                which rules out four of the ten common devices as soon as a pump is in the line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Use the driest month</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
                An annual average hides the only month that matters. A tank sized on 42 inches a year and
                emptied in the one month that gets half an inch was never sized at all — which is why the
                rainfall field here asks for a design month, not a yearly total.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== WORKED EXAMPLES ==== -->
      <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Worked examples</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600">
          A house, the one roof surface that changes the answer, and the comparison that surprises people.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-cyan-100 text-cyan-700 font-bold">
                1
              </span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">
                {fmt(DEFAULTS.areaSqFt, 0)} ft² asphalt roof, {fmt(DEFAULTS.drySpellDays, 0)}-day dry spell
              </h3>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-700">
              caught per inch = {fmt(DEFAULTS.areaSqFt, 0)} × {fmt(GAL_PER_SQFT_PER_INCH, 5)} × {fmt(DEFAULTS.coefficient, 2)} = {fmt(galPerInch, 0)} gal<br />
              supply that month = {fmt(base.supply, 0)} gal · demand = {fmt(base.periodDemand, 0)} gal<br />
              dry spell wants {fmt(DEFAULTS.demandGpd, 0)} × {fmt(DEFAULTS.drySpellDays, 0)} = {fmt(base.demandStorage, 0)} gal<br />
              roof can refill {fmt(base.yieldStorage, 0)} gal → smaller is{" "}
              <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(base.required, 0)} gal</span><br />
              → <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(base.selected ?? 0, 0)} gallon shell</span>, covers {fmt(base.daysCovered, 1)} days
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Result:</span> demand governs, with
              {" "}{fmt(periodBalance, 0)} gallons a month of surplus the tank simply cannot hold. That surplus
              is the argument for a bigger tank <em>or</em> for finding more uses — but not for a bigger roof.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-cyan-100 text-cyan-700 font-bold">
                2
              </span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">The same house with a green roof</h3>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-700">
              coefficient {fmt(0.85, 2)} → {fmt(0.3, 2)}<br />
              supply {fmt(base.supply, 0)} → <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(greenRoof.supply, 0)} gal</span><br />
              dry spell still wants {fmt(base.demandStorage, 0)} gal — now more than the roof gives<br />
              → governed by the <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">roof</span>, not the dry spell<br />
              → {fmt(base.selected ?? 0, 0)} gal shell drops to{" "}
              <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(greenRoof.selected ?? 0, 0)} gal</span>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Result:</span> a green roof is the only common
              surface that flips which rule governs. Every other material — metal, tile, membrane, even
              gravel — lands on the same tank, because the dry spell was always the binding limit. When the
              roof becomes the limit, more tank is wasted money and the fix is more catchment or less demand.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-cyan-100 text-cyan-700 font-bold">
                3
              </span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">Grey water against rainwater, same house</h3>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-700">
              grey water, 3 people = {fmt(grey.dailyYield, 1)} gpd ={" "}
              <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(greyAnnual, 0)} gal/yr</span><br />
              rainwater, 2,000 ft² at 42 in/yr = <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(rainAnnual, 0)} gal/yr</span><br />
              <span class="text-slate-500">— but in the driest month —</span><br />
              rainwater at 0.5 in = {fmt(rainDriestMonth, 0)} gal<br />
              grey water over 30 days = <span class="font-bold text-cyan-700">{fmt(greyThirtyDays, 0)} gal</span>{" "}
              = {fmt(driestRatio, 1)}× more
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
              <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Result:</span> over a year the two are within a few
              percent of each other. What separates them is timing — grey water arrives every single day,
              including the month the roof produces almost nothing, which is precisely the month irrigation
              demand peaks. Rainwater needs a tank because it is intermittent; grey water needs a field
              because it is not.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

